{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788132142,"nonce":"+rUh1IQygEzbwnapfFD/Y+IeEbM=","message":"CATALOG.PY GLYPH CROSS-CHECK — done, session 4, dorian's Linux box. This debt\nhas been carried since session 2 as \"diff the two glyph policies and reconcile\nthem BEFORE 3.2/3.3\". **Its premise was wrong, so there is nothing to\nreconcile — but there IS a real hazard, and it lands here on 2.5.**\n\n\n`rimworld-tools/baseviz/catalog.py:74`\n\n    def _glyph(defname: str) -\u003e str:\n        \"\"\"Short label drawn on a cell: drop a common prefix, take initials.\"\"\"\n        name = defname.split(\"_\", 1)[-1] if \"_\" in defname else defname\n        caps = [c for c in name if c.isupper()]\n        return (\"\".join(caps[:2]) if len(caps) \u003e= 2 else name[:2]).upper()\n\nvs `Source/AutoRimmer/Spatial.cs` `CropRenderer.ThingGlyph`/`PawnGlyph`.\n\nThey are not two implementations of one policy that drifted apart. They are two\nrenderers for two different media, and their constraints are opposite:\n\n| | baseviz `catalog.py` | AutoRimmer `CropRenderer` |\n|---|---|---|\n| output | 2-char UPPERCASE token | 1 ASCII char |\n| keyed on | the **defName** (`DiningChair` → `DC`) | def **properties** (category, passability, IsDoor, IsTree) |\n| medium | label drawn on a coloured, sized cell in a PNG | a text grid with nothing but the character |\n| collisions | don't matter — colour and size disambiguate | fatal — hence 2.6's reserved band `? @ $ ! \u0026 ^ ;` |\n\nSo \"reconcile the policies\" would have been the wrong move: making the ASCII\nchannel use `DC`-style 2-char tokens breaks a fixed-width grid, and making the\nPNG channel use single properties-derived chars throws away information the\nraster medium can afford to carry.\n\n\n1. **AutoRimmer does not read the catalog at all.** `grep -rn 'catalog\\|baseviz'\n   Source/AutoRimmer/*.cs` returns three COMMENTS and no code. 2.3's closing\n   note said \"colors stay the dumped baseviz_catalog.json's\", which reads as if\n   there were a dependency; there is none. The ASCII channel has no colours to\n   take.\n2. **The dumped `baseviz_catalog.json` contains no glyph field.** 3849 defs on\n   the bench, and the fields are `color, designationCategory, isStuff, kind,\n   label, mod, rotatable, size, stuffColor, stuffable, thingCategory`. The glyph\n   is computed in python at render time, never dumped. So there was never a\n   stored policy on the bench for the mod to diverge from.\n\n\n2.5 renders the same map through baseviz's canvas. The agent will then have\n**two visual channels with two different alphabets for the same cell** — `#` in\n`map-view`, a coloured cell labelled `WA` in the PNG. DESIGN wants the PNG to be\n\"an independent second visual check\", and independence is the point, so they do\nNOT have to match. But the agent must be told they are different alphabets\nrather than left to infer that `#` and `WA` are different things.\n\nConcretely, for whoever takes 2.5:\n- Do not introduce a THIRD alphabet. Use baseviz's as-is (that is the reuse the\n  spec asks for) or AutoRimmer's, and say which in the result payload.\n- Emit the channel's alphabet identity in the response — something a consumer\n  can key on — so 4.2 and 5.1 never compare a glyph from one channel against a\n  glyph from the other.\n- 2.6 changed the ASCII pawn band to `? @ $ ! \u0026 ^ ;` with `?` = fogged. If 2.5\n  draws pawns or fog at all, that is the set to be consistent with or explicitly\n  differ from.\n\n\n**The gate is lifted.** This was listed as blocking their viewport UX. It does\nnot: they consume `map-view`'s ASCII, which is self-consistent and now has an\nenforced reserved band, and they never touch catalog.py. 3.2/3.3 are clear on\nthis axis.\n\n2.3's closing-comment glyph table is stale (2.6 moved guest/tame/wild off\n`g`/`d`/`w`), but the authority is the code and its comment block in\n`Spatial.cs`, not that comment. Noted rather than edited — issue comments are a\nrecord of what was believed at the time.","files":null}]}